    "result_text": "A comment-only change; the full green suite already ran on the identical logic. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe request: when a create-owner request sets `sharesHousehold: true`, allow an owner at an address already used by a same-lastName owner, assign both the same stable `householdId`, and return it.\n\n**What I found already in place:** The V1 controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`) already skips the household-uniqueness check when `sharesHousehold` is true (that's what Cp10 exercises), and `sharesHousehold` already existed in the OpenAPI spec as a write-only field. What was missing was the `householdId` output.\n\n**Changes made:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` string property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 derive `householdId` deterministically from the owner's normalized (trimmed, whitespace-collapsed, lower-cased) last name + address via SHA-256, formatted `HH-XXXXXXXXXXXX`. Because it's derived from those two fields, any two owners sharing a household (same last name + address) automatically get the identical, stable id \u2014 satisfying \"assign both the same householdId\" with no ordering, storage, or backfill needed, and requiring no DB schema / seed-data changes.\n\n**Bug caught and fixed during testing:** My first cut put the normalization in a `default String normalize(String)` method on the mapper. MapStruct auto-detected that `String\u2192String` method as an implicit conversion and silently applied it to *every* String property \u2014 Cp05/Cp09 failed because names/addresses/codes came back lower-cased. Fixed by inlining the call through a `private` helper (MapStruct ignores private methods), so only `householdId` uses it.\n\n**Result:** `mvn test` \u2192 `Tests run: 21, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS, including the new Cp11 (`coreJoinersShareHouseholdId`) and the existing suite.",
